<p class="lede">Years before AwardHome had a single line of code, it had a domain
    name — bought on a promise I made to myself in my office, wondering who got
    what awards over the years while writing recommendation letters for my
    students.</p>

<p>I ran a studio. If you run one too, you already know the scene: a season's
    worth of trophies on the shelf, a phone full of award-ceremony photos, detailed results
    scattered across a dozen competition websites, PDFs, and Facebook posts — some
    already gone by summer. Every year, parents would ask me the same questions.
    <em>Where can I find the results from that regional in March? Didn't she win
        something at Nationals two years ago? Do you have a list of everything our
        team placed this season?</em>
</p>

<p>And every year, I did what studio owners everywhere do: I curated it by hand.
    Spreadsheets. Screenshots. Late nights matching "1st Overall — Junior Small
    Group" to the right eight kids, because the results sheet only listed the
    routine name. The dancers earned those moments on stage; keeping the record of
    them alive was somehow harder than the choreography.</p>

<p>It struck me that this was backwards. A dancer's competition history is a
    story — years of work, growth, teammates, teachers — and it deserved better
    than a shoebox of paper and a folder of screenshots. So I bought
    <strong>awardhome.com</strong> and made myself a promise: someday, every award
    would have a home.
</p>

<p>It took years to keep that promise. One research lab after another, one
    startup after another… as a parent of two kids, with many, many more kids to
    take care of in the studio, I could hardly find spare time — I
    procrastinated. Finally, with my own kids heading off to college, I started
    working on the promise: gathering and curating results the way a librarian
    gathers scattered manuscripts — event by event, season by season,
    competition by competition — and turning them into something families can
    actually hold onto. A permanent, beautiful, searchable trophy case and home
    page for every dancer I found — and for every studio, an award home page
    and tools of your own as well.</p>

<p>Today AwardHome holds over half a million awards, going back to 2011 — and
    it grows every week. Every one of them belongs to a dancer who earned it, a
    family who cheered for it, and a studio that made it possible. On
    September 15, we open the doors to everyone.</p>

<p>This platform is my thank-you to the dance community that gave me so much.
    I hope it feels like coming home.</p>